9:BACKGROUND: The high heterogeneity of depression prevents us from obtaining reproducible and de /uniFB01 nite anatomical maps of brain structural changes associated with the disorder, which limits the individualized diagnosis and treatment of patients. In this study, we investigated the clinical issues related to depression according to individual deviations from normative ranges of gray matter volume.
11:METHODS: We enrolled 1092 participants, including 187 patients with depression and 905 healthy control participants. Structural magnetic resonance imaging data of healthy control participants from the Human Connectome Project ( n = 510) and REST-meta-MDD Project ( n = 229) were used to establish a normative model across the life span in adults 18 to 65 years old for each brain region. Deviations from the normative range for 187 patients and 166 healthy control participants recruited from two local hospitals were captured as normative probability maps, which were used to identify the disease risk and treatment-related latent factors.
13:RESULTS: In contrast to case-control results, our normative modeling approach revealed highly individualized patterns of anatomic abnormalities in depressed patients (less than 11% extreme deviation overlapping for any regions). Based on our classi /uniFB01 cation framework, models trained with individual normative probability maps (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve range, 0.7146 -0.7836) showed better performance than models trained with original gray matter volume values (area under the receiver operating characteristic curve range, 0.6800 -0.7036), which was veri /uniFB01 ed in an independent external test set. Furthermore, different latent brain structural factors in relation to antidepressant treatment were revealed by a Bayesian model based on normative probability maps, suggesting distinct treatment response and inclination.
15:CONCLUSIONS: Capturing personalized deviations from a normative range could help in understanding the heterogeneous neurobiology of depression and thus guide clinical diagnosis and treatment of depression.
25:precise and personalized diagnosis and prognosis of depression (7,8). However, even though the brain structure is relatively stable, the results were inconsistent across studies when considering only decreased gray matter volume (GMV) in patients with depression compared with control participants (9,10). This might be partly explained by two factors. First, depression is a highly heterogeneous psychiatric syndrome (11 -13). DSM identi /uniFB01 es at least 256 unique symptom manifestations that meet the standard de /uniFB01 nition of depression, resulting in extensive clinical heterogeneity (14). Further, patients vary greatly in the temporal dynamics of symptom onset, including age of onset and frequency of episodes. Depression is also heterogeneous in psychiatric comorbidities and treatment outcomes (11,15). Second, the use of relatively small samples might lead to type I and II errors (16), therefore limiting the generalizability of results. These factors are inevitable challenges in the process of achieving precision medicine in depression.
27:The goal of precision medicine is to provide timely and accurate diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment for individual patients (17 -19). However, traditional case-control analyses allow only statistical inferences at the group level, rather than the individual level, and cannot fully capture brain abnormalities at the individual level (20,21). Furthermore, most studies employ standard single-variable analysis methods, the predictive power of which, either singularly or cumulatively, remains inadequate (22). Machine learning offers a new paradigm that enables automated and more objective assessment for prognosis and diagnosis of depression. Studies have shown that machine learning models could predict treatment-resistant depression with comparable accuracy (0.7) (23). Besides, data-driven subtypes of depression can facilitate an understanding of heterogeneity, which may help improve the predictability of diagnosis and clinical outcomes (24,25). Therefore, an approach that takes into account individual heterogeneity combined with machine learning algorithms is needed to understand the pathophysiological changes of depression and to explore whether there are potential diagnostic and antidepressant treatment-related effects.
29:Normative modeling is an emerging statistical technique for mapping individual deviations at the participant level with respect to a reference model, which could help in understanding the heterogeneous biology underlying psychiatric disorders (26,27). In this study, a large dataset of healthy adults 18 to 65 years of age from the Human Connectome Project (28) and REST-meta-MDD Project (29) was used to estimate normative models of GMV across the life span. These models were then applied to patient cohorts to assess the individual deviations from a normative range. Based on this, we developed three applications, as follows: 1) We built an individual depression prediction model in a discovery cohort to mine the depression-related information over those deviations from the normative range and provide an individualized objective assessment. We also explored the performance of the prediction model in an independent external test set. 2) A Bayesian model was used to explore the brain structural changes associated with antidepressant treatment to /uniFB01 nd the latent brain factors in the subgroup of patients who are sensitive to treatment with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs). 3) We further explored the similarities and differences in the brain structure of patients targeted by two typical antidepressants, i.e., SSRIs and serotonin norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (SNRIs). This study provides new insights into the neurobiological heterogeneity of depression and demonstrates its promising application in clinical diagnosis and treatment.
