## RESULTS

## Demographic and GMV Data

The demographic data of open datasets are shown in Table S2: 510 HC participants from the Human Connectome Project (mean age, 29.19 6 3.50 years; 302 women) and 229 HC participants from the REST-Meta-MDD Project (mean age, 39.34 6 15.66 years; 156 women). The demographic and clinical characteristics of participants in dataset I are summarized in Table 1. There were signi /uniFB01 cant age and sex differences between patients with depression and HC participants and between the discovery cohort and external test set, which was adjusted by our normative models. Furthermore, there was no signi /uniFB01 cant difference in the 17-item Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression score between responders and nonresponders at baseline ( p = .407) for patients treated with SSRIs (Table S3). The SSRI- and SNRI-targeted patients also had no signi /uniFB01 cant difference in the 17-item Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression score either at baseline ( p = .275) (Table S4) or after 4 weeks of treatment ( p = .257) (Table S4).

Figure 2A shows the group-level differences of GMV values between patients with depression and HC participants using the conventional case-control inference. For the 9 of 90 brain regions with signi /uniFB01 cant group differences in GMV, all were signi /uniFB01 cantly lower in the depression group compared with the HC group in the discovery cohort after false discovery rate correction ( p , .05) (Table S5). However, no positive groupwise signi /uniFB01 cant results were found in the external test set.

